.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt portgen_java 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm portgen_java
.Nd make a starter of a port for a Java program built using Maven.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm progname
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl k
.Op Fl r
.Ar source-directory
.Pp
Note that the programs invokes
.Xr doas 1
for the user _pfetch
to create the download directory, so you should arrange for this to be allowed without a password.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads the pom.xml file from the given source-directory
and generates some of the boilerplate of an OpenBSD ports Makefile.
.Pp
The normal workflow/process is to build your app manually in its source directory (with a pom file, with assembly:single),
then run this program pointed at that directory.
If it completes normally, cd to the target directory /usr/ports/mystuff/java/NAME.
Examine the directory for XXXes (grep -r XXX), then run 'make makesum package install'.
If that completes normally, create a startup script, either in bin or /etc/rc.d,
and add that to the files subdir and to the install rule.
.Sh FILES
/usr/ports/mystuff/java - port is created here, under the name field in the pom file.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
1 if the target directory exists.
.Sh BUGS
Maven will download its standard plugins and dependencies for each invocation of this program.
The "standard" plugins include many versions of many plugins, for a total of about 80 megabytes.
This may be slow on sub-terabyte links.
